            {
              "id": "glog-afb32f12-5d06-463e-beaa-256e2310292c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Vulnerable/Compromised Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of cryptographic hash functions that are considered weak or broken, making them susceptible to attacks such as collision attacks. In C++ programming, using a compromised hash algorithm like MD2 can lead to security vulnerabilities, as attackers may be able to generate different inputs that produce the same hash output, undermining the integrity of the data.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Avoid Using Weak Hash Functions**: Replace MD2 with a more secure hash function such as SHA-256 or SHA-3.\n2. **Use Trusted Libraries**: Utilize well-maintained cryptographic libraries that provide secure implementations of hash functions.\n3. **Regularly Update Dependencies**: Ensure that all cryptographic libraries are up-to-date to benefit from the latest security patches and improvements.\n4. **Conduct Security Audits**: Regularly review and audit code for the use of deprecated or insecure cryptographic algorithms.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is an example of how to replace the MD2 hash function with SHA-256 using the OpenSSL library in C++.\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <iomanip>\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\n// Function to compute SHA-256 hash\nstd::string computeSHA256(const std::string& input) {\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, input.c_str(), input.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for (int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; ++i) {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::string data = \"Example data\";\n    std::string hash = computeSHA256(data);\n    std::cout << \"SHA-256 Hash: \" << hash << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, you need the following library:\n\n- OpenSSL: A robust, full-featured open-source toolkit implementing the Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) and Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocols, as well as a full-strength general-purpose cryptography library.\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
