            {
              "id": "glog-b5b6cb1d-d182-44d1-bbb2-dd83d50a23ed",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source is larger than the destination. This is a common vulnerability in C++ programming that can lead to serious security issues such as data corruption, system crashes, and code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&result, vec.data(), sizeof(NumberType))`, the `memcpy` function is used to copy data from `vec.data()` to `result`. If the size of `vec.data()` is larger than the size of `result`, it can lead to buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the source and destination before using `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer alternative to `memcpy` is `std::copy` which is less prone to errors. Here is how you can modify the code:\n\n```cpp\nstd::copy(vec.begin(), vec.end(), std::back_inserter(result));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<vector>`: for the `vec` object.\n- `<algorithm>`: for the `std::copy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-1c3c798e-6962-4646-b9bf-3c5393299c80",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts characters until it encounters a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer over-read, information leakage, or even a crash (segmentation fault).\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(b))`, the `reinterpret_cast` is used to cast `b` to a `const char*`. If `b` is not a null-terminated string, this can lead to the vulnerabilities mentioned above.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. If you are dealing with raw bytes that may not be null-terminated, consider using functions that take a length parameter, such as `strnlen`, instead of `strlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `b` is a pointer to a buffer of known size `n`, you can use `strnlen` as follows:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // for strnlen\n\nsize_t len = strnlen(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(b), n);\n```\n\nIf `b` is a `std::vector<char>`, you can get the size directly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <vector> // for std::vector\n\nstd::vector<char> b;\n// ... fill b ...\nsize_t len = b.size();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for `strlen` or `strnlen`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-b975bf56-4953-4e91-9802-3af0f8642c29",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the source buffer is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, the `memcpy` function is copying the contents of `source` into `target` without checking if the size of `source` is larger than `target`. This can lead to a buffer overflow, which can cause the program to crash or allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied from the source buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source and destination buffers before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(target) >= sizeof(source)) {\n    memcpy(&target, &source, sizeof(source));\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source is too large to fit into target\n}\n```\n\nIn this version of the code, we first check if the size of `target` is larger or equal to the size of `source`. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy`. If it's not, we handle the error in a way that is appropriate for the specific program.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required for this code to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-6407be34-af86-4020-b179-6be49dce626f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the `counters_list_size` variable is user-controlled or can be manipulated, it can lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can occur if an attacker can cause more data to be written than space was allocated for, which can lead to overwriting other important data in memory.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust data coming from the user. Also, consider using functions that limit the amount of data that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\n// Assuming counters_list_size is an integer\nif(counters_list_size > 0 && counters_list_size <= MAX_SIZE) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(counters_list_size);\n    if(ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure (e.g., by exiting the program).\n    }\n    // Continue with program, free memory when done.\n    free(ptr);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `counters_list_size` is within a valid range (greater than 0 and less than or equal to some maximum size). Then, we check if `malloc` was successful by checking if the returned pointer is `NULL`. If `malloc` fails, we handle the failure appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-52dc3d85-3409-49ef-afff-81abdcbdfc3e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the source buffer and the size of the destination buffer are not properly checked. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&result, &x, sizeof(x))`, there is a potential risk if the size of `result` is less than the size of `x`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied from the source buffer. This can be done by comparing the sizes of the source and destination buffers before performing the `memcpy` operation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif(sizeof(result) >= sizeof(x)) {\n    memcpy(&result, &x, sizeof(x));\n} else {\n    // Handle error: destination buffer is not large enough\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first check if the size of `result` is greater than or equal to the size of `x`. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy` operation. If it is not, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so the only library dependency required by the code example is:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
