              "id": "glog-af313cb3-5072-4781-ac8b-9c87fe68fad8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check for the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source data is larger than the destination buffer. This is a common vulnerability in C++ programming that can lead to serious security issues such as data corruption, system crash, or even arbitrary code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `memcpy(gfxip, name_data, gfxip_label_len)`, the `memcpy` function is copying `gfxip_label_len` bytes from `name_data` to `gfxip`. If `gfxip_label_len` is larger than the size of `gfxip`, it will result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source data. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t gfxip_size = sizeof(gfxip);\nif (gfxip_label_len <= gfxip_size) {\n    memcpy(gfxip, name_data, gfxip_label_len);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source data is too large for destination buffer\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first get the size of `gfxip` using `sizeof`. Then we check if `gfxip_label_len` is less than or equal to `gfxip_size` before calling `memcpy`. If `gfxip_label_len` is larger than `gfxip_size`, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so you need to include the `<cstring>` header file in your code.\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
